[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Fragments and Colocation in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As GraphQL applications scale, organizing queries and managing data requirements across components becomes challenging. This tutorial explores GraphQL Fragments and Component Colocation, two architectural patterns that help maintain clean, modular, and performant codebases."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a GraphQL Fragment?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A GraphQL fragment is a piece of reusable query logic. It allows you to define a set of fields on a specific type and then include those fields in multiple queries, mutations, or other fragments. Fragments are particularly useful for breaking down complex data requirements into smaller, manageable parts."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"fragment UserFields on User {
  id
  name
  email
  avatarUrl
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Component Colocation"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Colocation is the practice of keeping code that changes together close to each other. In a component-based frontend architecture (like React, Vue, or Angular), component colocation means placing a UI component's GraphQL query or fragment directly inside the component file, rather than in a centralized location."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintainability: Developers can see exactly what data a component requires by looking at the component file itself."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Refactoring Safety: If you remove a component, its associated data requirements are removed simultaneously."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Only fetch the data that the currently rendered components actually need."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Colocated Fragments in Practice"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a UserProfile component that needs specific user details. Instead of writing a massive global query, the UserProfile component defines its own fragment."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import { gql } from '@apollo/client';

export const USER_PROFILE_FRAGMENT = gql`
  fragment UserProfileFields on User {
    id
    name
    bio
    avatarUrl
  }
`;

export function UserProfile({ user }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <img src={user.avatarUrl} alt={user.name} />
      <h2>{user.name}</h2>
      <p>{user.bio}</p>
    </div>
  );
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Higher-level parent components or page components can then compose these child fragments into a single, cohesive top-level query."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import { gql } from '@apollo/client';
import { USER_PROFILE_FRAGMENT, UserProfile } from './UserProfile';

export const PROFILE_PAGE_QUERY = gql`
  query GetProfilePage($userId: ID!) {
    user(id: $userId) {
      ...UserProfileFields
      recentPosts {
        id
        title
      }
    }
  }
  ${USER_PROFILE_FRAGMENT}
`;"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining GraphQL fragments with component colocation, you ensure that your frontend architecture remains scalable, predictable, and tightly coupled with your data layer. This pattern prevents over-fetching and keeps your component codebase clean as your application grows."}]
